      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^{2} + 2 x y&=y - 30\\\\x^{2} + 2 x y - y + 30&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y - y + 30$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x^{2} + 2 x y - y + 30)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x^{2} + 2 x y - y + 30)\\,j\\\\&=(2 x - 1)\\,i-(2 x + 2 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 2 x - 2 y&=0\\\\x&=-y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+(-1)y-(-30)&=0\\\\-y^{2}+(-1)y-(-30)&=0\\\\y^{2}-(-1)y+(-30)&=0\\\\(y-(-6))(y-(5))&=0\\\\y&=-6\\text{ or }5\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(6,-6)$ and $(-5,5)$."
    },
